"ieeexplore.ieee.org (https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6828714/)\nciteturn160view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/6828714/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3\nL0: # JavaScript is disabled\nL1: \nL2: In order to continue, we need to verify that you're not a robot. This requires JavaScript. Enable JavaScript and then reload the page.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn160view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8077601/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8077601/: Error fetching https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/document/8077601/\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://www.ieee-whispers.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/whispers_2014_technical_program.pdf)\nciteturn160view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 months ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 94; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.ieee-whispers.com/wp-content/uploads/2017/11/whispers_2014_technical_program.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 3881\nL0@P0: 6th Workshop on Hyperstectral Image and Signal Processing :\nL1@P0:  Evolution in Remote Sensing\nL2@P0: 24-27 June 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL3@P0: www.ieee-whispers.com\nL4@P0: Workshop Program\nL5@P0: Foreword\nL6@P0: Sponsors\nL7@P0: Technical sponsors\nL8@P0: Committees\nL9@P0: Conference Information\nL10@P0: Technical Program\nL11@P0: Plenary speakers\nL12@P0: Session menu\nL13@P0-2: Contents 2014\nL14@P2-4: Lausanne, SwitzerlandOpening of the conference\nL15@P4: 1. Foreword\nL16@P4: It is with great pleasure that we welcome you to Switzerland for this 6th\nL17@P4: edition of WHISPERS. Lausanne, city of Olympics, and the Ecole Polytech\u0002nique Fédérale de Lausanne are proud of hosting this workshop.\nL18@P4: This year, we have received the highest number of paper submissions\nL19@P4: in the history of WHISPERS, with 205 papers submitted from every corner of\nL20@P4: the planet (well, except maybe Antarctica). We wish to thank Jocelyn Chanus\u0002sot and Sebastian Lopez, the program chairs, for the incredible work done to\nL21@P4: gather all the reviews and to assemble an excellent technical program. We also\nL22@P4: would like to warmly thank all the reviewers, who dedicated their efforts and\nL23@P4: time to help improve contributions to WHISPERS!\nL24@P4: Following the tradition at WHISPERS, we offer a wide technical program of interest to a large com\u0002munity: WHISPERS will begin with four tutorials on contemporary and rapidly evolving topics relevant to the\nL25@P4: hyperspectral community (radiative transfer models, kernel methods, image processing and high performance\nL26@P4: computing). We would like to particularly thank the workshop tutors for their support: J. Verrelst, J. Moreno,\nL27@P4: G. Camps-Valls, A. Plaza, M. Fauvel and M. Dalla Mura.\nL28@P4: The tutorials will be followed by three days of technical sessions,\nL29@P4: which constitute the main body of the conference. Hydrology, fluorescence,\nL30@P4: planetary sciences and UAV: all these emerging topics join the more tra\u0002ditional technical sessions on unmixing, classification and modelling to\nL31@P4: provide a full experience for hyperspectral scientists and practitioners. Our\nL32@P4: gratitude also goes to the organizers of the special sessions: J. Theiler, A.\nL33@P4: Schaum, S. Amici, A. Lucieer, D. Odermatt, A. Wuest, H. Clenet, F. Tosi, M.\nL34@P4: Parente, R. Heylen, S. Delalieux, D. Messenger, D. Gillis, J. Mercier, R. Sun\u0002dberg, J. Verrelst and J. Moreno. They have helped us expand the horizon of\nL35@P4: the workshop to new areas and have ensured the presence of key scientists\nL36@P4: in their respective areas.\nL37@P4: And what would be hyperspectral imaging without an industrial\nL38@P4: component? This year we have a record number of sponsors, demonstrating a strong link between sensors,\nL39@P4: data providers and the scientific community. Do not forget to visit the expositors stands!\nL40@P4: In between sessions and discussions, we hope you will find the time to enjoy the beauty of the city of\nL41@P4: Lausanne and everything it has to offer. We hope you will enjoy it as much as we do.\nL42@P4: Welcome again to Lausanne and we wish you a rich and inspiring WHISPERS 2014!\nL43@P4: Devis Tuia and Michael Schaepman\nL44@P4: Back to the main menu\nL45@P4: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL46@P4-5: 5Opening of the conference\nL47@P5: On behalf of the IEEE Geoscience and Remote Sensing Society, I am\nL48@P5: delighted to welcome you to WHISPERS 2014, the 6th Workshop on Hy\u0002perspectral Image and Spectral Processing: Evolution in Remote Sensing.\nL49@P5: WHISPERS has developed into a premier international workshop for our\nL50@P5: community, both because of the quality of the research in hyperspectral fo\u0002cused algorithms, and the bridge between analysis and broad based applica\u0002tions in spectroscopy. As President of the GRSS for 2014, I want to express\nL51@P5: our appreciation to the General Co-Chairs, Devis Tuia (EPFL) and Michael\nL52@P5: Schaepman (U of Zurich), for hosting WHISPERS 2014 in Lausanne, and\nL53@P5: to program co-chairs Sebastian Lopez (Universidad de las Palmas de Gran\nL54@P5: Canaria) and Jocelyn Chanussot (Grenoble Institute of Technology) and to\nL55@P5: Frank de Morsier and Francois Golay (EPFL), of the organizing committee,\nL56@P5: which is critical to the success of the workshop.\nL57@P5: Interest in hyperspectral sensing and data analysis continues to\nL58@P5: grow in the international remote sensing community, as evidenced by both the submissions of papers to\nL59@P5: conferences and journals, and the increased interest in airborne and space-based hyperspectral sensing for a\nL60@P5: growing applications community. We anticipate the next generation of spaceborne missions, which will ad\u0002vance the international remote sensing spectral imaging community in many directions, and continue to be\nL61@P5: excited about opportunities for airborne sensing, including the rapidly growing interest in UAV technology.\nL62@P5: Parallel advances in spectral technology in food science, biomedical imaging, and environmental applica\u0002tions, to name a few, and methodology for analyzing hyperspectral data will also have a significant impact.\nL63@P5: These are great times to “think hyperspectral”.\nL64@P5: All the best from GRSS for a very successful WHISPERS 2014. I look forward to seeing you in Lausanne.\nL65@P5: Melba Crawford\nL66@P5: President, IEEE GRSS\nL67@P5: Back to the main menu\nL68@P5: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL69@P5-6: 6Opening of the conference\nL70@P6: 2. Exhibitors\nL71@P6: ReSe Applications Schläpfer is a small Swiss company focused on remote sensing data processing and imaging spec\u0002troscopy software. The company is focused in geometric and atmospheric modeling of radiation in optical remote sensing, as\nL72@P6: well as the geometric and radiometric preprocessing of optical remote sensing data. A development of ReSe is the PARGE® direct\nL73@P6: orthorectification software for airborne optical scanner systems. PARGE is used for both airborne imaging spectroscopy systems\nL74@P6: and for the rectification of UAV data.\nL75@P6: For scientific analyses of the radiative transfer in remote sensing, ReSe developed the MODO software for the handling\nL76@P6: of Modtran® radiative transfer calculations. With this regards, ReSe holds a license for the redistribution of the Modtran®4 and\nL77@P6: Modtran®5 software components as part of its products.\nL78@P6: ReSe successfully advances the development of the original ATCOR® atmospheric correction software packages in col\u0002laboration with Dr. R.Richter from DLR. ReSe holds exclusive rights for redistribution of the original implementation of the\nL79@P6: ATCOR software suite. ATCOR is suitable for standard satellite systems as well as for airborne scanner data. Recently, ReSe devel\u0002oped the BREFCOR BRDF correction module which is now included in ATCOR.\nL80@P6: HySpex, NEO’s line of hyperspectral cameras, aims to offer compact, high performance and versatile instru\u0002ments for a multitude of applications, ranging from airborne to laboratory and industrial use of imaging spectroscopy. Norsk\nL81@P6: Elektro Optikk AS (NEO) was established in 1985 as a privately owned research oriented company within the field of elec\u0002tro optics. NEO has grown to be the largest independent research and development organization in electro optics in Nor\u0002way, and has in addition established itself as a manufacturer of advanced electro optical products for an international market.\nL82@P6: Headwall Photonics is the leading designer and manufacturer of hyperspectral imaging spectrometers and spectral\nL83@P6: instrumentation for remote sensing research, military/defense, and industrial applications and markets. Hyperspec™ imaging\nL84@P6: spectrometer solutions are available for satellite, airborne, handheld and process control deployment. With exceptional optical\nL85@P6: efficiency, Headwall’s high performance spectrometers and spectral engines have been selected by OEM and end-user customers\nL86@P6: around the world for use in critical application environments. Headwall designs and manufactures patented aberration-corrected,\nL87@P6: spectral instrumentation that is customized for application-specific performance. Headwall Photonics was formed in 2003 as the\nL88@P6: result of a management buy-out from Agilent Technologies.\nL89@P6: Applications\nL90@P6: Schläpfer\nL91@P6: ReSe\nL92@P6: http://www.rese.ch\nL93@P6: http://www.hyspex.no\nL94@P6: http://www.HeadwallPhotonics.com\nL95@P6: Back to the main menu\nL96@P6: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL97@P6-7: 7Opening of the conference\nL98@P7: A Company specialized in the sale, distribution and service of Analytical Instrumentation, throughout France, Spain,\nL99@P7: Portugal and Switzerland. ASD´s exclusive representative.\nL100@P7: ASD Inc., a PANalytical company, is the global leader in remote sensing and hyperspectral measurement solutions,\nL101@P7: providing unparalleled ground truthing results. Our rugged, portable FieldSpec® 4 line of spectroradiometers provides the free\u0002dom to rapidly collect high-quality spectra in the field. Trusted by top research experts at thousands of universities and research\nL102@P7: institutions, ASD’s full-range spectrometers are used in more than 70 countries. For more information, please visit www.asdi.com.\nL103@P7: W. LUDOLPH founded in 1846 in Bremerhaven, Germany is a high standard manufacturer and approved service station\nL104@P7: for laboratory apparatus, navigation and scientific instruments. The company features different interrelated business divisions\nL105@P7: providing expertise in precision mechanics and special engineering. In cooperation with the German Alfred Wegener Institute\nL106@P7: Helmholtz Centre for Polar and Marine Research AWI we are the manufacturer and distributer of ManTIS, a new field spectro\u0002goniometer for ground based characterization of the surface. This is the appropriate tool for your spectro-directional observations\nL107@P7: and advanced use of hyperspectral sensors.\nL108@P7: Rikola Ltd. offers the world’s smallest and the most lightweight hyperspectral camera for UAVs. This novel, innovative\nL109@P7: product is a frame-based spectral system providing true snapshot images for all the wavelength bands at a time. The frame based\nL110@P7: approach enables an easy image stitching for the mosaics with high resolution images. In addition, the approach doesn’t need IMU\nL111@P7: for its operations, which makes the system low cost and low weight. The Rikola products have been tested with a wide variety of\nL112@P7: platforms including helicopters and fixed wing UAVs in several application areas like agriculture, forestry and water research. The\nL113@P7: camera system is very flexible, and it enables handheld use and use with computers in laboratory as well.\nL114@P7: SPECIM Spectral Imaging Ltd. is the world’s leading manufacturer of hyperspectral imaging instruments and systems.\nL115@P7: In addition to airborne remote sensing systems, SPECIM provides ImSpector Imaging Spectrographs, Spectral Cameras and Hy\u0002perspectral Imaging Solutions to an increasing range of demanding industrial and science applications like colour measurement,\nL116@P7: process analytical technology (PAT), life sciences, chemical imaging and forensics.\nL117@P7: http://www.bonsaiadvanced.com/\nL118@P7:  http://www.asdi.com\nL119@P7: http://www.ludolph.de\nL120@P7: http://www.rikola.fi\nL121@P7:  http://www.specim.fi\nL122@P7: Back to the main menu\nL123@P7: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL124@P7-8: 8Opening of the conference\nL125@P8: SphereOptics is a supplier of specialized equipment and services with a technical focus in the following areas of photon\u0002ics: light measurement, remote sensing and manufacturing of reflectance standards for the remote sensing and vision market.\nL126@P8: SphereOptics offers a wide product portfolio for the remote sensing market which ranges from field spectrometers, hyperspectal\nL127@P8: cameras, thermal - FTIR spectrometers to reflectance standards & targets and uniform light sources for calibration of imaging\nL128@P8: cameras.\nL129@P8: Since our formation in 2003, our goal has been to be much more than just a supplier of advanced equipment. Our mis\u0002sion is to SERVE BETTER. We do a few things really well, rather than try to do many things to a lesser standard. We serve our\nL130@P8: customers with expert advice and services. Our services include certified calibration of reflection standards, rent, lease and cali\u0002bration of remote sensing spectrometers & cameras. Our sales and application engineers have a strong technical background in\nL131@P8: remote sensing, geochemistry, agriculture, etc. They are just experts in these fields and help our customers to get the most out of\nL132@P8: their investments.\nL133@P8: The Cubert GmbH – Real Time Imaging Spectroscopy – Made in Germany\nL134@P8: Cubert is the first company in Europe, serving the market with a product that provides a real snapshot spectral imager.\nL135@P8: The technology was developed using no moving parts which makes it a very robust, high portable, lightweight camera. The imag\u0002ing rate goes up to 20 hyperspectral cubes per second, which allows the user to create hyperspectral video sequences. As there is\nL136@P8: a system that provides special and spectral data at the same time, data acquisition in imaging spectroscopy becomes much easier.\nL137@P8: In comparison to other imaging spectrometers, the Cubert system does not use a swivel head and a linear displacement\nL138@P8: unit, therefore it is possible to use the system mounted on very small portable devices without using additional technology, e.g.:\nL139@P8: gyroscopes.\nL140@P8: The technology platform allows a high spectral, spatial and temporal variability, which brings up a variety of new un\u0002explored areas of application. Therefore Cubert offers the possibility to create user adapted systems in addition to the standard\nL141@P8: products.\nL142@P8: http://www.sphereoptics.de\nL143@P8: http://cubert-gmbh.de\nL144@P8: Back to the main menu\nL145@P8: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL146@P8-9: 9Opening of the conference\nL147@P9: 3. Sponsors\nL148@P9: sarmap SA, a Swiss company founded in 1998, is active since its establishment in the development of data processing\nL149@P9: algorithms and operational land applications – including modelling – using spaceborne and airborne Synthetic Aperture Radar\nL150@P9: (SAR) and, more recently, spaceborne and UAV multi-spectral data. Capacity building are primarily offered to extend the un\u0002derstanding in the processing and use of remote sensing data, but also, to demonstrate the technical advantages and economical\nL151@P9: benefits of this technology. sarmap’s core activities are focused on the development of:\nL152@P9: •\t SARscape® and OPTICALscape®, a set of modules, embedded in ENVI®, for the processing of SAR and optical\nL153@P9:  multi-spectral data;\nL154@P9: •\t Digital Elevation Model based on airborne and spaceborne SAR systems, and, more recently, on the synergetic\nL155@P9:  use with stereo-optical data;\nL156@P9: •\t Land displacements for the measurement of Earth movements up to mm level;\nL157@P9: •\t Dedicated agricultural products targeted to food security, insurance, and precision farming applications at national scale;\nL158@P9: •\t Countrywide customized forest products primarily dedicated to certification and management purposes.\nL159@P9: SpecTIR, LLC Remote Sensing Experts\nL160@P9: SpecTIR is a small business concern based in Reno, Nev., with offices in Manassas, VA, Easton, MD, and Los Angeles,\nL161@P9: CA. The company features three interrelated business divisions providing expertise in all aspects of remote sensing, from the de\u0002sign of sensors to the development of imagery and GIS products.\nL162@P9: SpecTIR conducts a variety of large-scale geophysical and environmental studies. Projects include oil, gas, and mineral\nL163@P9: detection and exploration; point source pollution; water quality analysis and submerged aquatic vegetation mapping; forestry\nL164@P9: inventory and biomass evaluation and fire fuels modeling. With years of experience in remote sensing technology, SpecTIR’s\nL165@P9: innovative professionals perform these studies using airborne hyperspectral imagery to collect, process and interpret data. This\nL166@P9: technology is based on the unique fingerprint or spectral signatures reflected by objects in the form of waves of energy across\nL167@P9: the electromagnetic spectrum. Using very sensitive detectors, high-speed computers and large data storage capacities, SpecTIR’s\nL168@P9: professionals analyze this hyperspectral data to identify objects on the earth’s terrestrial and water surfaces.\nL169@P9: Raptor Photonics Limited is a global leader and manufacturer of high performance, industrial-grade and extremely\nL170@P9: rugged ultra low light digital & analogue cameras. Raptor specialises in complete cameras and core engine solutions using CCD,\nL171@P9: EMCCD, Scientific CMOS and SWIR sensor technology. The extreme low light capability of Raptor’s cameras makes them ideal\nL172@P9: for day/night surveillance, homeland security, hyperspectral imaging and scientific markets. Raptor Photonics Ltd is a registered\nL173@P9: ISO 9001:2008 company and is headquartered in Larne, Northern Ireland.\nL174@P9: http://www.sarmap.ch\nL175@P9: http://www.spectir.com\nL176@P9: ®\nL177@P9: http://www.raptorphotonics.com\nL178@P9: Back to the main menu\nL179@P9: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL180@P9-10: 10Opening of the conference\nL181@P10: Telops, located in Quebec City, Canada, specializes in the design and production of sophisticated opto-electronic sys\u0002tems for the defence & security, environmental and scientific research markets. Telops advanced optical systems allow the detec\u0002tion and identification of remote substances which are often invisible to the naked eye.\nL182@P10: In addition to providing specialized opto-electronic engineering services, Telops has also developed the Hyper-Cam,\nL183@P10: an infrared hyperspectral imager which allows standoff chemical detection at a distance of up to five kilometers. This advanced\nL184@P10: instrument enables its user to measure different spectrum and then compare the measured spectrum with the signatures of\nL185@P10: known gases and solids. The constituents and properties of a target can then be easily identified. As well Telops has developed a\nL186@P10: high performance line of infrared cameras which includes unique cameras, custom designed for specific applications. This line\nL187@P10: includes the FAST-IR comprised of two different rapid frame rate infrared cameras, the HD-IR which includes high-definition\nL188@P10: infrared cameras, the MS-IR which offers unique, multispectral capabilities, the HDR-IR, a high dynamic range camera which\nL189@P10: allows users to resolve scenes up to 2500°C and finally the TS-IR, a versatile, easy to use infrared camera which comes in an IP67\nL190@P10: sealed enclosure making it ideal for use in harsh environments.\nL191@P10: Telops also offers R&D services for optical system technology development. Telops experts deliver significant expertise\nL192@P10: in the fields of opto-electronic systems engineering with full disciplinary specialist in optical, mechanical, electronics, thermal,\nL193@P10: software and system engineering. Telops works closely with its clients/scientists to develop customized optical solutions in the\nL194@P10: area of infrared remote sensing, spectrometry, cryogenic and ruggedized optical systems as well as dedicated imaging and calibra\u0002tion systems.\nL195@P10: Perception Park is your partner for high performance data processing of Hyperspectral data. Whether you want to pro\u0002cess your data offline with highest efficiency or if you need a hard real time data processing solution, with the generic data process\u0002ing products of Perception Park you are always perfectly equipped to implement your solutions.\nL196@P10: Configurable real-time data processing:\nL197@P10: The intuitive configurable real-time data processing unit Perception System is mainly used in rough industrial fields like\nL198@P10: food processing, recycling, mining as well as pharmaceutical industries. By using Chemical Color Imaging, the Perception System\nL199@P10: allows that chemical and molecular information become useable for industrial image processing and machine vision. Now, part\u0002ners of Perception Park are able to provide new, highly innovative machinery to the market.\nL200@P10: Critical application environments such as high g-forces or low-energy cases are enabled by the implementation of an\nL201@P10: FPGA-based Perception System.\nL202@P10: First hyperspectral operating environment:\nL203@P10: To deploy hyperspectral technology nowadays is tricky and time-consuming. Especially the cooperation of involved\nL204@P10: experts of different disciplines is limited due to a missing common basis. Therefore Perception Park decided to develop a layer\nL205@P10: between the operating system and the end application - the first operating environment for hyperspectral technology.\nL206@P10: The included plug-in architecture provides a way to add or modify functionality. This results in an expandable and cus\u0002tomizable mutual basis for hyperspectral technology. Such concepts are known from web browsers or mobile operating systems\nL207@P10: (App). The operating environment supports real-time processing as well as the integration of hardware and software by plug-ins.\nL208@P10: Users can easily add own plugins to do things we never even thought of. An operating environment including a plug-in framework\nL209@P10: is simple, clean and efficient.\nL210@P10: http://www.telops.com\nL211@P10: color the invisible\nL212@P10: http://www.perception-park.com\nL213@P10: Back to the main menu\nL214@P10: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL215@P10-11: 11Opening of the conference\nL216@P11: Spectral Sciences, Inc. (SSI) is a research and development company dedicated to producing technical solutions from\nL217@P11: concept to prototype to meet the needs of its customers. SSI develops cutting-edge technologies for its clients, including aerospace\nL218@P11: industry-standard computer models, simulation and data analysis software, and unique instrument prototypes. SSI scientists have\nL219@P11: expertise in radiative transfer, physical modeling, and the development of spectral data analysis tools.\nL220@P11: - Software for Remote Sensing, Spectroscopy and Imaging SSI scientists are authors of widely recognized computer codes\nL221@P11: for modeling, simulating and processing atmospheric and space electro-optic radiation environments in three dimensions. MOD\u0002TRAN®5 radiative transfer model, FLAASH and QUAC atmospheric correction software (sold together as the ENVI Atmospheric\nL222@P11: Correction Module), and MCScene and QUID spectral image scene simulation software are all SSI products.\nL223@P11: - Spectral Sensing & Imaging Instrumentation SSI has developed a rugged, adaptable technology product line for ground-\nL224@P11: , aircraft-and space-based spectral imaging, control of aero-turbine combustion, and ambient gas and surface monitoring. Ad\u0002ditional sensor systems employ optical gas correlation, tunable diode-lasers, and absorption monitoring.\nL225@P11: - Optical Phenomenology in Targets and Backgrounds SSI scientists are leading experts in modeling optical signatures\nL226@P11: from aircraft, rockets, and non-equilibrium sources such as rocket plumes, vapor clouds, and chemical releases.\nL227@P11: - Advanced Imaging Techniques SSI is applying tomographic reconstruction techniques adapted from radiotherapy to\nL228@P11: non-destructive testing, concealed object detection, and combustion monitoring concepts.\nL229@P11: ITRES (1979) is an airborne hyperspectral remote sensing imager manufacturer and worldwide mapping survey provider.\nL230@P11: Our performance-designed custom hyperspectral imagers are lidar-ready and feature unmatched precision, focus, and\nL231@P11: resolution. VNIR, SWIR, MWIR, & thermal IR spectral regions covered for infrastructure and environmental applications. Also\nL232@P11: offered: multiple sensor operation, remote control.\nL233@P11: Recent product announcements: Wide-array thermal TABI-1800. Halve survey costs with a mapping swath twice as\nL234@P11: wide as the closest competitor. Applications include mapping heat loss, effluent, aquatic habitat, search and rescue, and thermal\nL235@P11: anomaly detection.\nL236@P11: IPS (In-Flight Processing System): Rapid response mapping & data turnaround through in-flight geocorrection, mo\u0002saicking, target/thermal anomaly detection.\nL237@P11: DigitalGlobe is a leading provider of commercial high-resolution earth observation and advanced geospatial solutions\nL238@P11: that help decision makers better understand our changing planet in order to save lives, resources and time. Sourced from the\nL239@P11: world’s leading constellation, our imagery solutions deliver unmatched coverage and capacity to meet our customers’ most de\u0002manding mission requirements. Each day customers in defense and intelligence, public safety, civil agencies, map making and\nL240@P11: analysis, environmental monitoring, oil and gas exploration, infrastructure management, navigation technology, and providers of\nL241@P11: location-based services depend on DigitalGlobe data, information, technology and expertise to gain actionable insight.\nL242@P11: DigitalGlobe is a registered trademark of DigitalGlobe.\nL243@P11: http://spectral.com\nL244@P11: airborne hyperspectral & thermal remote sensing\nL245@P11: http://www.itres.com\nL246@P11: http://www.digitalglobe.com\nL247@P11: Back to the main menu\nL248@P11: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL249@P11-12: 12Opening of the conference\nL250@P12: 4. Technical sponsors\nL251@P12: http://www.ieee.org http://www.grss-ieee.org\nL252@P12: http://www.epfl.ch http://www.snf.ch\nL253@P12: http://space.epfl.ch http://www.vaud.ch\nL254@P12: http://www.lausanne.ch http://www.gipsa-lab.grenoble-inp.fr\nL255@P12: Back to the main menu\nL256@P12: Whispers Conference 2014, Lausanne, Switzerland\nL257@P12-13: 13Opening of the conference\nL258@P13: 5. Committees\nL259@P13: General Chair\nL260@P13: Devis Tuia, EPFL Lausanne, Switzerland\nL261@P13: M. Schaepman, University of Zurich, Switzerland\nL262@P13: Program Chairs\nL263@P13: Jocelyn Chanussot, Grenoble Institute of Technology, France\nL264@P13: Sebastian Lopez, Universidad de las Palmas de Gran Canaria, Spain\nL265@P13: Organizing Committee\nL266@P13: Devis Tuia, EPFL Lausanne, Switzerland\nL267@P13: Frank de Morsier, EPFL Lausanne, Switzerland\nL268@P13: François Golay, EPFL Lausanne, Switzerland\nL269@P13: Technical Committee\nL270@P13: Peter Bajorski, Rochester Institute of Technology, USA\nL271@P13: Jon Atli Benediktsson, University of Iceland, Iceland\nL272@P13: Jose Bioucas Dias, Technical University of Lisbon, Portugal\nL273@P13: Xavier Briottet, ONERA, Toulouse, France\nL274@P13: Lorenzo Bruzzone, University of Trento, Italy\nL275@P13: Melba Crawford, Purdue University, USA\nL276@P13: Sylvain Douté, Laboratoire de Planètologie de Grenoble, France\nL277@P13: Jenny Q. Du, Mississippi State University, USA\nL278@P13: Peijun Du, Nanjing University, P.R. China\nL279@P13: Bethany L. Ehlmann, Caltech, USA\nL280@P13: Paolo Gamba, University of Pavia, Italy\nL281@P13: David Goodenough, University of Victoria, Canada\nL282@P13: Bormin Huang, University of Wisconsin-Madison, USA\nL283@P13: Xiuping Jia, Australian Defence Force Academy, Canberra, Australia\nL284@P13: John Kerekes, Rochester Institute of Technology, USA\nL285@P13: Erzsébet Merényi, Rice University, USA\nL286@P13: Muhammad Murtaza Khan, NUST-SEECS University, Pakistan\nL287@P13: Nasser Nasrabadi, U.S. Army Research Laboratory, USA\nL288@P13: Mario Parente, Brown University, USA\nL289@P13: Antonio Plaza, University of Extremadura, Spain\nL290@P13: Ils Reusen, VITO, Belgium\nL291@P13: John Richards, The Australian National University, Australia\nL292@P13: Stanley Rotman, Ben-Gurion University of the Negev, Israel\nL293@P13: Alan Schaum, Naval Research Laboratory, Washington, D.C, U